introduction:
  bigQuestion: "How do we apply computer science to the unknown?"
  overview: "This unit prepares you for <strong>Paper 1 Section B</strong>. Each year, the IB releases a 'Case Study' scenario describing a fictional organization facing a new technological challenge (e.g., Rescue Robots, Automated Airports). Your job is to research the emerging technology and propose strategic solutions."
  journey: "You will start by analyzing the <strong>Scenario</strong> to identify stakeholders and problems. Then you'll conduct <strong>Research</strong> into the specific technology (outside the standard syllabus). Finally, you'll practice answering the <strong>Challenge Questions</strong>, justifying your strategy with evidence."

concepts:
  - title: "Scenario Analysis"
    icon: "fa-magnifying-glass"
    content: |
      <p>The Case Study is a 6-page booklet. You must unpack it line-by-line:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Stakeholders:</strong> Who is affected? (Operators, Customers, Public).</li>
          <li><strong>Current System:</strong> What is broken or outdated?</li>
          <li><strong>New Tech:</strong> What is being proposed? (e.g., vSLAM, Lidar, Blockchain).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Syllabus Integration"
    icon: "fa-puzzle-piece"
    content: |
      <p>You must link the Case Study back to what you know:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Theme A:</strong> How does the network/hardware support this?</li>
          <li><strong>Theme B:</strong> What algorithms/logic are required?</li>
          <li><strong>Integration:</strong> How does standard theory explain this new tech?</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Strategic Evaluation"
    icon: "fa-chess"
    content: |
      <p>The final question is an essay. You must:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Evaluate:</strong> Weigh the pros and cons of a decision.</li>
          <li><strong>Justify:</strong> Explain WHY your solution is best.</li>
          <li><strong>Synthesize:</strong> Combine case facts with your research.</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "Case Study"
    definition: "A document detailing a specific scenario involving emerging technology, used for Paper 1 Section B."
  - term: "Primary Research"
    definition: "Information you gather yourself (e.g., testing similar software, interviewing experts)."
  - term: "Secondary Research"
    definition: "Information from existing sources (e.g., academic papers, tech news, specifications)."
  - term: "Challenge Question"
    definition: "The final question in the exam which asks for a strategic solution to a complex problem."
  - term: "Justification"
    definition: "Providing valid reasons or evidence to support an opinion or conclusion."
  - term: "Emerging Technology"
    definition: "Tech that is currently developing or will be developed in the next 5-10 years (e.g., Quantum, AR, AI Swarms)."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "General Knowledge"
    correction: "Writing generic answers like 'It makes it faster'. You need SPECIFIC details about the specific tech in the case study."
  - mistake: "Listing vs Evaluating"
    correction: "Listing 10 features of the tech is not an evaluation. You must explain the IMPACT of those features on the stakeholders."
  - mistake: "Ignoring Constraints"
    correction: "Suggesting a solution that is too expensive or technically impossible given the scenario's limits."

syllabusPoints:
